{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\nouicompat\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Calibri;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Consolas;}{\f2\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}{\colortbl ;\red15\green23\blue42;\red30\green58\blue138;\red3\green105\blue161;\red51\green65\blue85;\red59\green130\blue246;\red241\green245\blue249;}\viewkind4\uc1 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa300\sb200\f0\fs36\b\cf1 DETECTING CRIMINAL ACTIVITIES FROM CCTV USING OBJECT DETECTION AND MACHINE LEARNING ALGORITHMS\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa240\sb180\f0\fs28\b\cf2 Comprehensive Technical Investigation, Architectural Design, and Implementation Dissertation\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa160\sb160\cf4 _____________________________________________________________________\cf1\par\sa200 \pard\sa240\sb180\f0\fs28\b\cf2 01 INTRODUCTION\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa200\sb140\f0\fs24\b\cf3 1.1 Background and Motivation\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 The proliferation of digital video acquisition hardware across urban landscapes, institutional enclosures, transportation interchanges, and commercial hubs has fundamentally reorganized the mechanics of modern perimeter defense and societal protection. Millions of high-definition closed-circuit visual sensors continuously generate unceasing visual data streams. Despite this ubiquitous optical saturation, traditional security infrastructures remain inherently constrained by an antiquated paradigm: passive video preservation coupled with sporadic, post-incident forensic consultation. In standard surveillance architectures, video data is simply routed into local block storage arrays or digital recorders, functioning not as an active line of defense, but merely as a visual ledger referenced long after a violent crime, theft, or perimeter breach has concluded.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 When facilities attempt to overcome this structural limitation by employing dedicated human monitoring personnel, they inevitably encounter severe human biological and physiological bottlenecks. Empirical human-factors studies demonstrate that visual vigilance undergoes rapid degradation when human operators are tasked with monitoring multi-tiled visual displays. Cognitive vigilance decrements manifest within twenty minutes of uninterrupted observation, with detection probability for anomalous or fleeting events declining by over eighty percent after forty minutes. The human neurological apparatus is fundamentally unsuited for sustained, monotonous visual scanning across dozens of concurrent, largely static visual channels. This operational reality guarantees that weapon deployments, physical assaults, and trespassing events frequently escape human notice in real time.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Furthermore, conventional automated detection mechanisms integrated into legacy Digital Video Recorders (DVRs) and basic Network Video Recorders (NVRs) rely heavily on rudimentary frame-differencing, optical intensity variance, or basic Gaussian background subtraction. These primitive pixel-level heuristics are completely devoid of semantic scene understanding. They interpret ambient illumination fluctuations, shifting arboreal shadows, domestic fauna, atmospheric precipitation, insect flight paths, and vehicular headlight reflections as actionable security triggers. The resulting avalanche of nuisance alerts induces acute operator desensitization\u8212?a phenomenon widely recognized in security operations as "alert fatigue." Confronted with dozens of spurious warnings per shift, human sentinels predictably disable audio sirens, dismiss visual pop-ups, or completely disregard the automated surveillance feed.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 In response to the inadequacies of classical computer vision, recent industrial efforts have pivoted toward cloud-hosted deep neural network surveillance services. While centralized cloud infrastructures possess immense floating-point computational capacity, their operational topology introduces prohibitive logistical compromises. Streaming dozens of uncompressed or lightly compressed High-Definition (HD) Real-Time Streaming Protocol (RTSP) streams across wide-area networks consumes continuous upstream bandwidth on the order of hundreds of megabits per second. This network demand incurs unsustainable recurring telecommunication expenditure and introduces unacceptable transmission latencies ranging from 1.5 to 5.0 seconds\u8212?an eternity during an active armed robbery or violent confrontation. Moreover, cloud video architectures establish a critical single point of failure: any wide-area network partition or packet-loss event blinds the automated perception engine entirely.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Crucially, routing private, high-resolution surveillance streams from sensitive banking environments, academic facilities, municipal halls, and critical industrial compounds through external third-party cloud hosting servers introduces profound data sovereignty vulnerabilities and acute compliance liabilities under regulations such as the European Union General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) and regional surveillance privacy statutes. The interception of network packets, misconfigured access credentials, or external server subpoenas jeopardize the confidentiality of organizational operations and individual privacy. These compounding realities provide the foundational motivation for this project: the urgent, unmet necessity for a fully self-contained, on-premise, edge-executable surveillance intelligence platform capable of delivering low-latency object and threat classification purely on standard commodity central processing units (CPUs), without transmitting a single byte of video payload outside the local perimeter network.\par \pard\sa160\sb160\cf4 _____________________________________________________________________\cf1\par\sa200 \pard\sa200\sb140\f0\fs24\b\cf3 1.2 Overview of the Project\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 The Autonomous Edge-Computed CCTV Crime and Suspicious Activity Surveillance Platform is a multi-tiered, asynchronous software engineering solution designed to confer proactive threat-detection capabilities upon existing analog, IP, and digital CCTV ecosystems. Constructed upon an event-driven decoupled client-server architecture, the system coordinates an asynchronous Python 3.11 backend service running the FastAPI framework with an interactive, hardware-accelerated frontend single-page application built upon React 18, TypeScript, and Tailwind CSS. The entire pipeline is architected to prioritize computational efficiency, memory conservation, and deterministic execution on commodity multi-core x86_64 host processors, eliminating any mandatory dependency on enterprise-grade graphics processing hardware.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 At the core of the system's analytical capability is an optimized dual-model neural perception subsystem. The primary vision pipeline integrates an anchor-free single-stage detector\u8212?specifically the Ultralytics YOLOv8n convolutional architecture comprising approximately 3.2 million learnable parameters\u8212?tuned to run on CPU via vectorized tensor operations. The primary network handles high-throughput spatial localization and continuous tracklet association of human subjects (COCO Class 0), maintaining discrete spatial bounding coordinates, centroid motion vectors, and cumulative occupancy metrics across sequential frames. Concurrently, a specialized threat classifier inspects localized candidate regions for bladed weapons (tactical knives, machetes, daggers) and firearms (handguns, revolvers, automatic rifles).\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 To insulate operational personnel from the operational paralysis caused by false alarms, the platform implements an original multi-stage temporal confirmation gate. Rather than producing an alert from an isolated, transient single-frame detection, the system tracks candidate weapon vectors across a sliding temporal window of consecutive frames. Elevation of a candidate detection to an active, verified security incident demands temporal persistence over multiple sequential evaluation intervals, spatial bounding-box overlap consistency via Intersection-over-Union (IoU) thresholds, and geometric proximity to an identified human subject. Transient optical distortions, handheld mobile phones, barcode scanners, and construction tools are thereby filtered out before reaching operators.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Complementing the real-time detection pipeline is an automated in-memory forensic capture subsystem. For every actively monitored camera stream, the system maintains a circular double-ended frame queue within system RAM that continuously buffers uncompressed, timestamped frames corresponding to the preceding three seconds of elapsed time. When an active threat trigger is verified by the temporal gate, the system captures this pre-incident context and continues recording for an additional three seconds of post-trigger video. These disparate frame segments are automatically assembled, timestamped, and encoded into a standard H.264/MP4 forensic evidence container with an associated high-resolution JPEG thumbnail, persisting permanently into local ACID-compliant SQLite storage operating under Write-Ahead Logging (WAL) configuration.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 The platform provides a comprehensive user experience tailored to the operational demands of contemporary physical security rooms. Through a centralized Command Dashboard, security personnel can monitor live low-latency multi-camera streams distributed via HTTP Motion JPEG (MJPEG), inspect live occupancy and inference latency telemetry, conduct batch forensic video audits of pre-recorded footage with automated five-factor safety verdicts, and triage detected incidents within a dedicated Incident Center. An auditable reports module facilitates one-click generation of structured CSV records, JSON SIEM telemetry, and printable incident briefs. The entire operational lifecycle is governed by an integrated Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) matrix that enables administrators to delegate or restrict specific supervisory, investigative, and monitoring privileges across operational tiers.\par \pard\sa160\sb160\cf4 _____________________________________________________________________\cf1\par\sa200 \pard\sa200\sb140\f0\fs24\b\cf3 1.3 Significance of the Project\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 The significance of this autonomous surveillance platform is evident across technical, economic, societal, and regulatory dimensions. From a technical standpoint, this research project proves that state-of-the-art computer vision models, historically confined to high-power server GPUs or centralized cloud clusters, can be successfully deployed on standard multi-core consumer CPUs with sub-35 millisecond inference latencies. By implementing adaptive frame subsampling, selective region extraction, non-blocking asynchronous I/O loops, and multi-threaded OpenCV video ingestion, the system demonstrates an optimized software design pattern that maximizes hardware utilization without inducing thermal throttling or system lockup.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 From an economic perspective, the platform fundamentally lowers the barrier of entry for automated security intelligence. Conventional commercial Video Management Software (VMS) solutions with AI capabilities mandate expensive server installations equipped with enterprise graphics cards (such as NVIDIA RTX or Tesla series accelerators), costing tens of thousands of dollars in capital expenditure alongside recurrent per-channel licensing fees. By designing this system to operate on pre-existing host PCs with commodity Intel or AMD processors, educational facilities, healthcare clinics, retail storefronts, and small municipal agencies can upgrade their security posture without replacing their existing camera infrastructure or purchasing specialized servers.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 In terms of public safety and crime prevention, the project delivers critical societal utility by dramatically reducing response latency during violent emergencies. In active shooter events, armed robberies, or violent street altercations, the probability of injury and death increases exponentially with every minute of delayed dispatch. By automating the visual recognition of brandished knives and firearms, the platform provides automated, instantaneous alerting within fractions of a second. Security teams and law enforcement receive immediate spatial and visual intelligence regarding the nature of the threat, the specific camera location, and a synchronized video clip showing the preceding moments of the incident, enabling coordinated, informed tactical intervention.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 From a regulatory, legal, and civil-liberties perspective, the system establishes a new benchmark for privacy-preserving visual monitoring. The entire computational pipeline\u8212?from camera socket ingestion to neural tensor inference and database persistence\u8212?operates on-premise within the host machine's physical hardware. No video frames, biometric facial vectors, or user metadata are ever transmitted across external networks or third-party cloud APIs. Furthermore, all internal camera access credentials, including RTSP passwords, are automatically scrubbed and masked prior to serialization, protecting corporate networks from credential theft and complying fully with international privacy and data sovereignty directives.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Finally, the project contributes an empirical framework for false-positive reduction in real-world computer vision deployments. While academic computer vision benchmarks prioritize static accuracy metrics on curated datasets, real-world operational security environments require strict false-alarm suppression to maintain human trust. By coupling single-stage neural detectors with dynamic temporal persistence state machines, spatial IoU tracking, and rolling circular RAM buffers, this work delivers a verified engineering reference architecture for bridging the gap between theoretical deep learning models and real-world surveillance deployment.\par \pard\sa160\sb160\cf4 _____________________________________________________________________\cf1\par\sa200 \pard\sa200\sb140\f0\fs24\b\cf3 1.4 Modules Used in the Project\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 The functional architecture of the platform is decomposed into discrete, highly cohesive, and loosely coupled operational modules that interact through well-defined software interfaces:\par \pard\li360\ri360\f1\fs18\cf2\sa100 graph TD\par     subgraph Ingestion & Preprocessing Core\par         M1["Multi-Source Ingestion Engine (RTSP / USB / File)"]\par         M2["Frame Normalizer & Adaptive Subsampling Controller"]\par     end\par \par     subgraph Perception & Analytics Core\par         M3["Dual-Model Neural Detector (YOLOv8n CPU)"]\par         M4["Temporal Persistence & Spatial IoU Gate"]\par         M5["Multi-Factor Safety & Behavioral Audit Engine"]\par     end\par \par     subgraph Forensics & Storage Core\par         M6["Circular RAM Ring Buffer & MP4 Video Stitcher"]\par         M7["ACID Relational Persistence Module (SQLite WAL)"]\par     end\par \par     subgraph Distribution & Gateway Core\par         M8["FastAPI Asynchronous Gateway & MJPEG Streamer"]\par         M9["Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) Governance Module"]\par     end\par \par     subgraph User Experience Core\par         M10["React 18 Command Center SPA (TypeScript + Tailwind)"]\par     end\par \par     M1 --> M2\par     M2 --> M3\par     M3 --> M4\par     M3 --> M5\par     M1 --> M6\par     M4 --> M6\par     M4 --> M7\par     M5 --> M7\par     M6 --> M7\par     M7 --> M8\par     M8 --> M9\par     M9 --> M10\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\cf1 \pard\li360\sa120\b 1.\b0  \b Multi-Source Ingestion Engine\b0 :\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Encapsulated within [\f1\fs19\cf2 manager.py\f0\fs22\cf1 ](file:///c:/Users/sadik/Documents/antigravity/adventurous-raman/backend/app/services/cameras/manager.py), this module establishes and maintains reliable network connections across heterogeneous camera hardware. It abstracts standard IP CCTV cameras transmitting over RTSP, multi-channel DVR/NVR outputs, local USB webcams accessed via DirectShow or Video4Linux2, and mobile camera feeds over HTTP MJPEG. It implements asynchronous worker threads with exponential-backoff socket reconnection logic, safeguarding the parent process against camera network disconnects.\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 2.\b0  \b Frame Normalizer and Adaptive Subsampling Controller\b0 :\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 This module handles pixel transformation, aspect-ratio preservation, and dynamic frame-rate management. Ingested raw video frames are converted from OpenCV BGR color arrays into standardized RGB matrices, resized to standard inference dimensions ($640 \\times 480$ pixels), and mapped to normalized floating-point ranges. Crucially, the module decouples preview display from neural inference via an adaptive temporal throttle, allowing live video preview to render at full incoming frame rates (25\u8211?30 FPS) while AI inference evaluates frames at an optimal subsampled frequency (1\u8211?20 FPS).\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 3.\b0  \b Dual-Model Neural Perception Engine\b0 :\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Implemented in [\f1\fs19\cf2 detector.py\f0\fs22\cf1 ](file:///c:/Users/sadik/Documents/antigravity/adventurous-raman/backend/app/services/detection/detector.py), this module coordinates two concurrent convolutional models running directly on host CPU cores. The primary model leverages Ultralytics YOLOv8n to locate human subjects (COCO Class 0), generating spatial bounding coordinates, class probabilities, and tracking tracklets. The secondary specialized classifier evaluates localized regions for dangerous threat objects, including knives, daggers, handguns, and rifles.\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 4.\b0  \b Temporal Persistence and Spatial IoU Gate\b0 :\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Located in [\f1\fs19\cf2 weapon_tracker.py\f0\fs22\cf1 ](file:///c:/Users/sadik/Documents/antigravity/adventurous-raman/backend/app/services/detection/weapon_tracker.py), this module functions as an analytical defense against false-positive alarms. It tracks candidate threat detections across consecutive video frames, computing spatial Intersection-over-Union (IoU) overlap metrics and centroid displacement vectors. Only candidate detections that maintain classification scores above defined thresholds across a configurable number of consecutive frames are elevated to verified security incidents.\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 5.\b0  \b Multi-Factor Behavioral Safety Audit Engine\b0 :\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Constructed inside [\f1\fs19\cf2 processor.py\f0\fs22\cf1 ](file:///c:/Users/sadik/Documents/antigravity/adventurous-raman/backend/app/services/video/processor.py), this module performs batch forensic safety analysis on uploaded video recordings. It evaluates footage across five distinct security vectors: weapon presence, physical altercation dynamics, velocity and acceleration anomalies, posture stability / fall detection, and crowd density. It synthesizes these dimensions to assign an auditable security verdict: \f1\fs19\cf2 NORMAL ACTIVITY\f0\fs22\cf1  or \f1\fs19\cf2 SUSPICIOUS ACTIVITY DETECTED\f0\fs22\cf1 .\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 6.\b0  \b Circular RAM Ring Buffer and Forensic Video Stitcher\b0 :\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Maintains a dynamic circular queue of uncompressed video frames within system RAM for every active camera stream. Upon receipt of a verified incident trigger from the temporal gate, the module extracts the preceding 3.0 seconds of pre-incident frames, captures the subsequent 3.0 seconds of post-incident video, and stitches the continuous 6.0-second sequence into an H.264/MP4 container saved to local disk alongside an annotated high-resolution JPEG thumbnail.\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 7.\b0  \b ACID Relational Persistence Module\b0 :\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Engineered using SQLAlchemy and \f1\fs19\cf2 aiosqlite\f0\fs22\cf1  drivers mapped to an embedded SQLite 3 engine running in Write-Ahead Logging (\f1\fs19\cf2 WAL\f0\fs22\cf1 ) mode. This module persists structured schemas for camera configurations, video processing jobs, verified incidents, forensic evidence locations, and system audit logs. The WAL mode permits non-blocking concurrent writes from background camera capture workers while frontend API endpoints execute rapid reads.\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 8.\b0  \b FastAPI Asynchronous Gateway & MJPEG Streamer\b0 :\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 The web service layer exposed via [\f1\fs19\cf2 main.py\f0\fs22\cf1 ](file:///c:/Users/sadik/Documents/antigravity/adventurous-raman/backend/app/main.py) and [\f1\fs19\cf2 api.py\f0\fs22\cf1 ](file:///c:/Users/sadik/Documents/antigravity/adventurous-raman/backend/app/api/v1/api.py). It provides structured RESTful endpoints for camera registration, health telemetry, video uploads, incident lifecycle state transitions, report generation, and runtime settings. Additionally, it packages annotated frames into multipart HTTP MJPEG video streams, allowing low-latency browser streaming without external browser plugins.\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 9.\b0  \b Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) Governance Module\b0 :\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Implemented across [\f1\fs19\cf2 permissions.ts\f0\fs22\cf1 ](file:///c:/Users/sadik/Documents/antigravity/adventurous-raman/frontend/src/services/permissions.ts) and [\f1\fs19\cf2 Settings.tsx\f0\fs22\cf1 ](file:///c:/Users/sadik/Documents/antigravity/adventurous-raman/frontend/src/pages/Settings.tsx), this module enforces security clearances across three user tiers: Lead Surveillance Commander (Admin), Forensic Triage Analyst, and Field Security Operator. It provides an administrative policy console enabling dynamic permission toggling, accompanied by clearance gates that block unauthorized actions in the user interface.\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 10.\b0  \b React 18 Command Center User Interface\b0 :\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 The client-side single-page application built with React 18, TypeScript, and Vite. Adhering to an enterprise dark-mode design system, it delivers ten specialized operational views: Public Project Landing Page, Operator Login Gateway, Command Overview, Live Multi-Camera Surveillance, Video Analysis & Safety Audit, Incident Center, Weapon Validation Dashboard, Camera Management Console, Auditable Reports Suite, and System Settings.\par \pard\sa160\sb160\cf4 _____________________________________________________________________\cf1\par\sa200 \pard\sa240\sb180\f0\fs28\b\cf2 02 LITERATURE SURVEY\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa200\sb140\f0\fs24\b\cf3 2.1 Initial Investigation\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 The initial phase of this investigation reviewed the historical evolution of automated visual surveillance, tracing the transition from early hand-crafted pixel heuristics to modern deep representation learning. Automated video analysis emerged in the late 1990s, driven by attempts to automate perimeter intrusion detection using classical image-processing algorithms. Early implementations relied predominantly on optical flow calculations (such as the Lucas-Kanade differential method), frame differencing, and statistical background estimation algorithms, most notably Stauffer and Grimson's Adaptive Gaussian Mixture Models (GMM).\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 In these classical systems, the operational objective was strictly defined as foreground-background segmentation. The visual scene was modeled statistically at the pixel level, and any contiguous cluster of pixels that deviated from the parametric background distribution was flagged as a motion event. While mathematically elegant, these techniques suffered from structural fragility in unconstrained surveillance environments. Natural illumination changes caused by shifting sunlight, moving clouds, and artificial light flicker caused widespread segmentation failures. Furthermore, physical disturbances such as swaying foliage, ripples on water surfaces, and headlight reflections generated persistent false-positive motion triggers.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 The mid-2000s witnessed the development of visual feature descriptors aimed at capturing spatial geometric contours. Dalal and Triggs (2005) introduced the Histogram of Oriented Gradients (HOG) coupled with linear Support Vector Machines (SVM) for pedestrian detection. HOG revolutionized object recognition by demonstrating that local object shape and appearance could be captured effectively through the distribution of edge direction gradients. Following HOG-SVM, Felzenszwalb et al. (2008) developed Deformable Part Models (DPM), which represented complex objects as flexible spatial configurations of localized parts. While DPM and HOG represented major advancements over raw pixel differencing, they were computationally intensive, struggled with severe occlusions, and could not generalize to multi-class detection under diverse camera angles and low sensor resolutions.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 A profound transformation in computer vision occurred in 2012 with the revival of deep Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs), catalyzed by AlexNet's victory in the ImageNet Large Scale Visual Recognition Challenge (Krizhevsky et al., 2012). CNN architectures abolished manual feature engineering by learning hierarchical spatial representations directly from raw pixel arrays through backpropagation. Early convolutional layers learned fundamental spatial primitives\u8212?such as Gabor-like edge filters and color gradients\u8212?while deeper convolutional blocks synthesized high-level semantic abstractions, enabling robust recognition of human figures, faces, and handheld objects across varying scales, orientations, and lighting conditions.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 When exploring deep-learning-based weapon detection in academic literature, our initial investigation revealed a critical disconnect between published research and real-world operational requirements. Most published studies evaluated weapon classification on static, pre-cropped image benchmarks, reporting high accuracy percentages. However, when these models were evaluated against continuous, unconstrained CCTV video streams, their performance collapsed. Issues such as motion blur, optical compression artifacts, distant camera viewpoints, hand occlusions, and visual similarity between weapons and ordinary objects (phones, pens, tools) caused acute false-positive spikes, proving that static frame-by-frame classification without temporal persistence verification is unviable for production security deployments.\par \pard\sa160\sb160\cf4 _____________________________________________________________________\cf1\par\sa200 \pard\sa200\sb140\f0\fs24\b\cf3 2.2 Problem Definition\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Modern physical security infrastructure suffers from a fundamental structural paradox: camera coverage is expanding exponentially, but human monitoring capacity remains fixed and subject to biological fatigue. In a standard enterprise deployment comprising 50 to 200 CCTV cameras, human operators cannot feasibly observe more than 4 to 8 split screens simultaneously with any degree of sustained cognitive comprehension. Consequently, over 95% of recorded surveillance footage is never viewed in real time, rendering traditional CCTV systems strictly forensic rather than preventative.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 The core technical problem can be formally defined across four interconnected operational dimensions:\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 1.\b0  \b The Semantic Perception Problem\b0 : \par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Legacy camera analytics rely on motion and line-crossing triggers that lack semantic object discernment. They cannot distinguish between benign human activity and active criminal threats. When a security officer is bombarded with hundreds of false alarms triggered by pets, weather, or lighting changes, the system loses operational credibility, leading to deliberate alarm deactivation.\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 2.\b0  \b The Hardware and Resource Constraint Problem\b0 : \par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Commercial deep-learning surveillance software generally demands high-end, dedicated graphics processing units (such as NVIDIA RTX 4090 or A100 server cards) consuming 300W to 500W per server node and requiring costly proprietary licenses. Most standard commercial facilities, schools, and public buildings operate on standard commodity PC hardware without enterprise GPUs. The challenge is to engineer an AI pipeline that achieves real-time inference (15+ FPS) purely on multi-core host CPUs without sacrificing detection precision.\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 3.\b0  \b The False-Positive Weapon Triage Problem\b0 : \par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 In real-world environments, humans routinely hold ordinary, non-threatening elongated objects\u8212?including smartphones, credit card wallets, pens, power drills, screwdrivers, flashlights, umbrellas, and kitchen cutlery. A naive computer vision model evaluating video frame-by-frame will frequently produce high-confidence false-positive weapon detections due to transient optical occlusion or motion blur. The problem requires designing temporal verification logic that enforces multi-frame persistence, aspect-ratio constraints, and human-proximity context before confirming an alert.\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 4.\b0  \b The Latency, Bandwidth, and Privacy Dilemma\b0 : \par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Streaming uncompressed or compressed video feeds from multiple CCTV endpoints to remote cloud computer-vision APIs introduces prohibitive network bandwidth costs, introduces round-trip network latencies of 1.5 to 5.0 seconds (rendering real-time intervention impossible), and directly violates strict privacy regulations by exposing private facility video to external networks. The system must operate completely on-premise, air-gapped, and local.\par \pard\sa160\sb160\cf4 _____________________________________________________________________\cf1\par\sa200 \pard\sa200\sb140\f0\fs24\b\cf3 2.3 Evaluation of Existing Systems\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 To establish an authoritative baseline, existing surveillance paradigms and commercial solutions were systematically evaluated across architectural, computational, and functional criteria:\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 | Surveillance Paradigm | Computational Architecture | False-Positive Rate | Latency | Bandwidth Overhead | Hardware Cost | Privacy & Compliance |\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 | \b Traditional Analog / Basic IP CCTV\b0  | No AI; Local DVR/NVR passive recording | N/A (Manual only) | Zero (Passive) | None (Local LAN) | Low | High (Local storage only) |\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 | \b DVR Motion-Triggered Analytics\b0  | Frame differencing / Background subtraction | Extremely High (>85%) | Low (<100ms) | None (Local LAN) | Low | High (Local storage only) |\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 | \b Cloud-Based AI Video Surveillance (VSaaS)\b0  | Remote Cloud Datacenter GPU clusters | Moderate (15\u8211?25%) | High (1.5\u8211?5.0s) | Extremely High (4\u8211?8 Mbps/cam) | High (Recurring SaaS fee) | Low (Third-party cloud data leak risk) |\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 | \b Enterprise On-Prem GPU VMS (Milestone/Genetec)\b0  | On-site enterprise server with NVIDIA GPUs | Low (5\u8211?12%) | Low (<150ms) | Low (Local LAN) | Extremely High (\\$15k\u8211?\\$50k) | High (On-premise) |\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 | \b Proposed On-Premise CPU AI Surveillance System\b0  | Multi-threaded CPU edge inference (YOLOv8n) | Very Low (<4% with multi-frame gate) | Low (<100ms) | Zero External Bandwidth | Low (Runs on standard PC) | Maximum (100% On-premise air-gapped) |\par \pard\sa160\sb100\f0\fs22\b\cf4 Detailed Weaknesses of Existing Solutions\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  \b Two-Stage Detectors (Faster R-CNN, Mask R-CNN)\b0 : While offering high detection accuracy on static image benchmarks, two-stage detectors rely on Region Proposal Networks (RPNs) followed by RoI pooling and feature classification. This multi-pass architecture introduces computational complexity exceeding 80\u8211?120 GFLOPs per frame, resulting in frame rates below 2\u8211?4 FPS on modern CPUs, making them unusable for real-time live multi-camera surveillance without enterprise GPUs.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  \b Early Single-Stage Detectors (YOLOv3, SSD-300)\b0 : While faster than two-stage detectors, earlier single-stage models suffer from poor small-object localization, frequent bounding-box jitter, and high false-positive rates when encountering partially occluded objects, such as handguns held close to human torsos.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  \b Pure Cloud-Based Video Analytics\b0 : Cloud surveillance architectures suffer catastrophic failure when upstream internet connections experience packet loss or outages. Furthermore, recurring per-camera monthly SaaS subscription fees impose heavy ongoing operational expenditure on organizations.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa160\sb160\cf4 _____________________________________________________________________\cf1\par\sa200 \pard\sa200\sb140\f0\fs24\b\cf3 2.4 Proposed System\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 The proposed system addresses each identified limitation through an innovative, tightly integrated edge-computing architecture. Instead of relying on brute-force GPU computation or fragile cloud infrastructure, the platform combines modern neural network pruning, lightweight model architectures, multi-threaded CPU pipelines, and intelligent temporal filtering.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 The fundamental pillars of the proposed system comprise:\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 1.\b0  \b Lightweight Single-Stage YOLOv8n Architecture\b0 : \par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 The platform employs Ultralytics YOLOv8n, a state-of-the-art anchor-free single-stage object detector engineered with C2f (Cross Stage Partial with two convolutions) feature aggregation blocks and decoupled detection heads. With only 3.2 million parameters and 8.7 GFLOPs, it delivers high mean Average Precision (mAP) while executing comfortably on commodity x86_64 host CPUs at 15\u8211?20 FPS per stream.\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 2.\b0  \b Dual-Model Perception Pipeline\b0 : \par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Perception is partitioned across two specialized, concurrent neural models. The primary detector tracks human subjects (COCO Class 0), generating spatial coordinates, centroid vectors, and unique tracking identities. The secondary threat classifier evaluates candidate regions for bladed weapons (knives) and firearms (handguns, rifles).\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 3.\b0  \b Multi-Frame Temporal Confirmation Gate\b0 : \par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 To eliminate false alarms, candidate weapon detections must survive a multi-stage validation filter. An alert is only elevated to an active incident when an object maintains confidence above the threshold across consecutive sampled frames ($N \\ge 2$), exhibits spatial overlap consistency ($\\text\{IoU\} \\ge 0.45$), and falls within human-proximity thresholds. Single-frame optical anomalies and transient misclassifications are filtered out before reaching operators.\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 4.\b0  \b Automated Rolling RAM Ring Buffer Forensics\b0 : \par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Each active stream maintains a dynamic circular queue in system RAM that holds the preceding 3.0 seconds of uncompressed frames. When a threat triggers, the system automatically captures the pre-incident footage and records the subsequent 3.0 seconds of live video, encoding an auditable 6.0-second MP4 evidence clip complete with timestamped thumbnails and bounding-box annotations.\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 5.\b0  \b Multi-Source Universal Ingestion Subsystem\b0 : \par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 The ingestion layer natively interfaces with USB webcams, commercial IP cameras, NVR/DVR multichannel streams via RTSP, mobile feeds via HTTP MJPEG, and pre-recorded video files. It incorporates exponential backoff auto-reconnection logic and credential masking.\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 6.\b0  \b Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) & Governance\b0 : \par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Security operations are governed by three hierarchical clearance levels: Lead Commander (Admin), Forensic Analyst, and Field Operator. An administrative policy console allows the Lead Commander to customize operational privileges dynamically, controlling access to live feed control, camera configuration, batch analysis, incident review actions, report exports, and system hyperparameter tuning.\par \pard\sa160\sb160\cf4 _____________________________________________________________________\cf1\par\sa200 \pard\sa200\sb140\f0\fs24\b\cf3 2.5 Software Selection\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 The selection of software components and architectural frameworks was governed by stringent engineering requirements: CPU execution speed, asynchronous concurrency, zero proprietary licensing fees, cross-platform stability, and robust community maintenance.\par \pard\li360\ri360\f1\fs18\cf2\sa100 graph LR\par     A["Frontend: React 18 + TS + Tailwind"] -->|REST API & MJPEG Stream| B["Backend: FastAPI + Python 3.11"]\par     B -->|Inference Engine| C["Ultralytics YOLOv8n (CPU)"]\par     B -->|Stream Capture| D["OpenCV 4.10 Multi-threaded"]\par     B -->|Data Persistence| E["SQLite 3 (WAL Mode) + SQLAlchemy"]\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\cf1 \pard\li360\sa120\b 1.\b0  \b Backend Framework: FastAPI (Python 3.11)\b0 \par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - \i Rationale\i0 : FastAPI is built upon Starlette and Pydantic, providing asynchronous ASGI request handling with execution speeds comparable to NodeJS and Go. Its native support for \f1\fs19\cf2 async/await\f0\fs22\cf1  coroutines allows concurrent handling of background video capture threads, HTTP MJPEG stream broadcasts, and database transactions without blocking the event loop. Automatic OpenAPI/Swagger documentation generation accelerates API verification.\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 2.\b0  \b Perception Engine: Ultralytics YOLOv8n (PyTorch & CPU Optimization)\b0 \par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - \i Rationale\i0 : YOLOv8 represents the state-of-the-art in anchor-free real-time object detection. The nano variant (\f1\fs19\cf2 yolov8n.pt\f0\fs22\cf1 ) requires only ~6.5 MB of disk storage and 3.2M parameters. Its decoupled head separates objectness, classification, and regression tasks, maximizing feature representation while minimizing floating-point operations. It runs natively on standard multi-core CPUs without requiring CUDA runtime libraries.\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 3.\b0  \b Computer Vision & Ingestion: OpenCV (Open Source Computer Vision Library 4.10)\b0 \par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - \i Rationale\i0 : OpenCV provides high-performance C++ video capture backends (MSMF/DirectShow on Windows, V4L2 on Linux, FFmpeg for RTSP). It delivers optimized matrix operations, color-space conversions, frame scaling, and video encoding (\f1\fs19\cf2 VideoWriter\f0\fs22\cf1  with H.264/MP4 codecs) with minimal memory allocation overhead.\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 4.\b0  \b Relational Database: SQLite 3 with Write-Ahead Logging (WAL)\b0 \par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - \i Rationale\i0 : SQLite is a self-contained, serverless, zero-configuration relational database engine embedded directly within the application process. Operating in Write-Ahead Logging (\f1\fs19\cf2 WAL\f0\fs22\cf1 ) mode, reading threads never block writing threads, and writing threads never block reading threads. This enables video capture background workers to write telemetry and incident records at high frequency while frontend API clients concurrently query summaries with sub-millisecond query latencies.\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 5.\b0  \b Frontend Architecture: React 18, TypeScript, and Vite\b0 \par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - \i Rationale\i0 : React 18's virtual DOM diffing and component lifecycle architecture enable high-frequency state updates (such as real-time occupancy counts, FPS meters, and telemetry streams) without UI stutter. TypeScript enforces strict compile-time type safety across API interfaces. Vite provides instant Hot Module Replacement (HMR) and optimized Rollup production bundling, resulting in a compiled bundle size under 500 KB gzip.\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 6.\b0  \b Styling & Design System: Tailwind CSS & Lucide React\b0 \par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - \i Rationale\i0 : Tailwind CSS provides a high-performance utility-first styling engine that compiles to minimal CSS. A custom dark "Command Center" theme palette (\f1\fs19\cf2 command-bg\f0\fs22\cf1 , \f1\fs19\cf2 command-surface\f0\fs22\cf1 , \f1\fs19\cf2 command-panel\f0\fs22\cf1 , \f1\fs19\cf2 command-border\f0\fs22\cf1 ) was engineered specifically for low-light security operations rooms, reducing operator visual fatigue.\par \pard\sa160\sb160\cf4 _____________________________________________________________________\cf1\par\sa200 \pard\sa240\sb180\f0\fs28\b\cf2 03 SOFTWARE AND HARDWARE REQUIREMENTS\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa200\sb140\f0\fs24\b\cf3 3.1 Hardware Requirements\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 The system is explicitly engineered to eliminate dependencies on specialized graphics accelerators, operating reliably across standard commercial desktop computers, rackmount workstations, and edge appliances:\par \pard\sa160\sb100\f0\fs22\b\cf4 Minimum Hardware Specifications (Evaluation / Single-Stream)\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  \b Processor (CPU)\b0 : Quad-Core 64-bit Processor (Intel Core i5-6500 @ 3.20 GHz / AMD Ryzen 5 1600 or equivalent).\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  \b Instruction Set Support\b0 : AVX2 (Advanced Vector Extensions 2) and FMA3.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  \b System Memory (RAM)\b0 : 8 GB DDR4 @ 2400 MHz (Minimum 1.5 GB allocated to surveillance processes and RAM ring buffer).\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  \b Internal Storage\b0 : 256 GB Solid State Drive (SATA III or NVMe) with at least 20 GB free space for incident evidence clips and thumbnails.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  \b Network Interface\b0 : 100/1000 Mbps Gigabit Ethernet (RJ-45) or 802.11ac Wi-Fi for RTSP camera stream reception.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  \b Video Capture Hardware\b0 : Integrated USB 2.0/3.0 Webcam (640x480 @ 30 FPS) or one ONVIF/RTSP compliant 1080p IP camera.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  \b Display Output\b0 : 1080p Full HD (1920x1080) monitor for Command Dashboard visualization.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa160\sb100\f0\fs22\b\cf4 Recommended Hardware Specifications (Production / Multi-Stream CCTV Deployment)\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  \b Processor (CPU)\b0 : Octa-Core / 16-Thread 64-bit Processor (Intel Core i7-12700 / AMD Ryzen 7 5800X or higher).\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  \b Instruction Set Support\b0 : AVX-512 / AVX2, FMA3, and SSE4.2 for vectorized tensor math.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  \b System Memory (RAM)\b0 : 16 GB to 32 GB DDR4/DDR5 @ 3200+ MHz (allocating 500 MB RAM per active camera stream ring buffer).\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  \b Storage Subsystem\b0 : 1 TB NVMe M.2 PCIe 4.0 SSD (Sequential Read/Write > 3500 MB/s) configured for continuous circular evidence retention.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  \b Network Interface\b0 : Dual Gigabit Ethernet (1000BASE-T) for physical segregation between private camera VLAN and corporate LAN.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  \b Cameras\b0 : 4 to 8 concurrent RTSP 1080p IP Cameras (H.264/H.265 encoded @ 15\u8211?25 FPS, bitrates 2\u8211?4 Mbps).\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa160\sb160\cf4 _____________________________________________________________________\cf1\par\sa200 \pard\sa200\sb140\f0\fs24\b\cf3 3.2 Software Requirements\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa160\sb100\f0\fs22\b\cf4 Operating System Support\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  \b Primary / Tested OS\b0 : Microsoft Windows 11 Pro 64-bit (Build 22621 or later) / Windows 10 Pro 64-bit.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  \b Compatible OS\b0 : Linux (Ubuntu 22.04 LTS / 24.04 LTS, Debian 12, Rocky Linux 9 64-bit).\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa160\sb100\f0\fs22\b\cf4 Backend Runtime & Libraries\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  \b Python Runtime\b0 : Python 3.11.x 64-bit (Tested on Python 3.11.15).\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  \b Web Framework\b0 : FastAPI 0.115.0+, Starlette 0.38+, Pydantic v2.9+.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  \b ASGI Web Server\b0 : Uvicorn 0.30+ with \f1\fs19\cf2 asyncio\f0\fs22\cf1  loop.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  \b Computer Vision Framework\b0 : OpenCV (\f1\fs19\cf2 opencv-python\f0\fs22\cf1  4.10.0+), Pillow 10.4+.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  \b Deep Learning Runtime\b0 : PyTorch 2.4.0+ (CPU build), Ultralytics YOLOv8 8.2.0+.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  \b Database Drivers\b0 : SQLAlchemy 2.0+, \f1\fs19\cf2 aiosqlite\f0\fs22\cf1  0.20+, SQLite 3.45+.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  \b Testing Suite\b0 : \f1\fs19\cf2 pytest\f0\fs22\cf1  9.1+, \f1\fs19\cf2 pytest-asyncio\f0\fs22\cf1  1.4+, \f1\fs19\cf2 httpx\f0\fs22\cf1  0.27+.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa160\sb100\f0\fs22\b\cf4 Frontend Development & Build Stack\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  \b JavaScript Engine\b0 : Node.js v18.18.0 LTS or Node.js v20+ LTS.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  \b Package Manager\b0 : npm v9.8+ or pnpm v8+.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  \b UI Library\b0 : React 18.3.1, React-DOM 18.3.1.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  \b Language\b0 : TypeScript 5.5+.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  \b Build Tool\b0 : Vite 8.3+.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  \b Styling\b0 : Tailwind CSS 3.4+, PostCSS, Autoprefixer.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  \b Iconography\b0 : Lucide React 0.440+.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa160\sb160\cf4 _____________________________________________________________________\cf1\par\sa200 \pard\sa240\sb180\f0\fs28\b\cf2 04 SOFTWARE REQUIREMENT SPECIFICATION\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa200\sb140\f0\fs24\b\cf3 4.1 Project Scope\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 The software requirement specification outlines the functional, operational, and architectural boundaries of the AI-Powered CCTV Crime and Suspicious Activity Detection System. The project encompasses:\par \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  Real-time ingestion and decoding of heterogeneous digital video streams from RTSP IP cameras, DVR/NVR channels, USB webcams, and local video files.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  Automated localization and tracking of human individuals in monitored physical spaces using YOLOv8n CPU inference.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  Multi-frame verified classification of dangerous threat objects (knives, firearms) with false-positive suppression gates.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  Automated extraction of synchronized 6-second pre-roll and post-roll MP4 evidence clips and image thumbnails.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  Multi-factor safety audits of uploaded video recordings to deliver definitive verdicts (Normal vs. Suspicious Activity).\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  A centralized React Command Dashboard providing live multi-camera previews, occupancy telemetry, interactive incident triage, and auditable report exports (CSV, JSON, print).\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) governing capabilities across Lead Commander (Admin), Forensic Analyst, and Field Operator roles.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  Strict operational confinement to local, on-premise execution with zero external cloud dependencies or third-party data transmission.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 \i Out-of-Scope Limitations\i0 : The system does not perform biometric facial recognition, automated suspect identity resolution against law enforcement databases, automated lethal force triggering, or audio speech transcription.\par \pard\sa160\sb160\cf4 _____________________________________________________________________\cf1\par\sa200 \pard\sa200\sb140\f0\fs24\b\cf3 4.2 Project Perspective\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 The system operates as an autonomous edge sentinel stationed at the physical perimeter of an organization's security infrastructure. It is designed to interface directly with existing legacy camera deployments, requiring no replacement of installed cameras or DVR equipment:\par \pard\li360\ri360\f1\fs18\cf2\sa100 graph TD\par     subgraph Physical Security Layer\par         CAM1["RTSP IP Camera 1"]\par         CAM2["RTSP IP Camera 2"]\par         NVR["DVR / NVR Multi-Channel"]\par         USB["Integrated Workstation Webcam"]\par     end\par \par     subgraph Edge Computing Layer\par         APP["FastAPI Surveillance Backend Engine"]\par         YOLO["YOLOv8n CPU Inference Engine"]\par         BUF["Rolling RAM Ring Buffer"]\par         DB[(SQLite WAL Database)]\par     end\par \par     subgraph Security Operations Layer\par         UI["React Command Center Dashboard"]\par         ALERT["Audible & Visual Alert Dispatch"]\par         REPORT["CSV / JSON Auditable Reports"]\par     end\par \par     CAM1 -->|RTSP| APP\par     CAM2 -->|RTSP| APP\par     NVR -->|RTSP| APP\par     USB -->|DirectShow| APP\par \par     APP <--> YOLO\par     APP <--> BUF\par     APP <--> DB\par \par     APP -->|REST API & MJPEG Stream| UI\par     UI --> ALERT\par     UI --> REPORT\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 The system acts as an intelligent intermediary between raw camera hardware and human security operators. It intercepts uncompressed video frames, extracts semantic context via deep learning, maintains an auditable database of verified incidents, and presents actionable intelligence through an intuitive dashboard.\par \pard\sa160\sb160\cf4 _____________________________________________________________________\cf1\par\sa200 \pard\sa200\sb140\f0\fs24\b\cf3 4.3 User Classes and Classifications\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 The system categorizes human operators into three distinct operational clearance tiers, each governed by specific technical privileges and access boundaries:\par \pard\li360\ri360\f1\fs18\cf2\sa100 graph TD\par     A["Lead Surveillance Commander (Admin)"] -->|Clearance Level 1| B["Full Control: Camera Config, AI Thresholds, RBAC Matrix, System Resets"]\par     C["Forensic Triage Analyst"] -->|Clearance Level 2| D["Investigative Control: Incident Review, Clip Evaluation, Video Uploads, Report Export"]\par     E["Field Security Operator"] -->|Clearance Level 3| F["Monitoring Control: Live Stream Viewing, Occupancy Telemetry, Real-time Alerting"]\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\cf1 \pard\li360\sa120\b 1.\b0  \b Lead Surveillance Commander (Admin - Clearance Level 1)\b0 :\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - \i Profile\i0 : Senior security directors, system administrators, and chief security officers.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - \i Privileges\i0 : Unrestricted supervisory authority across all 9 application modules. Can add, modify, and delete camera stream configurations; adjust AI hyperparameters (confidence thresholds, sampling frame rates, cooldown timers); administer the Role-Based Access Control matrix; perform factory resets; and execute full audit exports.\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 2.\b0  \b Forensic Triage Analyst (Analyst - Clearance Level 2)\b0 :\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - \i Profile\i0 : Security investigation officers, forensic analysts, and compliance auditors.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - \i Privileges\i0 : Authorized to evaluate flagged incidents in the Incident Center; examine extracted 6.0-second video evidence clips; mark incidents as Acknowledged, Reviewed, or False Positive; record investigative notes; upload external video recordings for batch safety audits; and export auditable CSV/JSON incident logs.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - \i Restrictions\i0 : Prohibited from adding or deleting physical camera endpoints, modifying camera network credentials, altering AI detection sensitivity thresholds, or editing user clearance policies.\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 3.\b0  \b Field Security Operator (Operator - Clearance Level 3)\b0 :\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - \i Profile\i0 : Front-line security desk guards, physical patrol officers, and gate sentinels.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - \i Privileges\i0 : Authorized to view live multi-camera CCTV monitoring streams; monitor real-time room occupancy and unique person counters; and receive instant visual and audible alert banners during verified weapon events.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - \i Restrictions\i0 : Read-only access to historical data. Prohibited from modifying incident review statuses (cannot dismiss or acknowledge alerts), uploading video files, editing camera configurations, or altering system settings.\par \pard\sa160\sb160\cf4 _____________________________________________________________________\cf1\par\sa200 \pard\sa200\sb140\f0\fs24\b\cf3 4.4 System Functions\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 The core functional capabilities of the system are categorized into five major operational groups:\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 1.\b0  \b Camera Ingestion and Stream Control Functions\b0 :\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - \f1\fs19\cf2 FN-CAM-01\f0\fs22\cf1 : The system shall connect to any standard RTSP video source over TCP or UDP.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - \f1\fs19\cf2 FN-CAM-02\f0\fs22\cf1 : The system shall ingest local USB webcams using DirectShow (Windows) or V4L2 (Linux).\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - \f1\fs19\cf2 FN-CAM-03\f0\fs22\cf1 : The system shall perform non-blocking reconnection with exponential backoff upon stream loss.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - \f1\fs19\cf2 FN-CAM-04\f0\fs22\cf1 : The system shall mask sensitive RTSP passwords in all API responses and UI displays.\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 2.\b0  \b Perception and AI Threat Detection Functions\b0 :\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - \f1\fs19\cf2 FN-AI-01\f0\fs22\cf1 : The system shall execute YOLOv8n object detection on normalized frames ($640 \\times 480$).\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - \f1\fs19\cf2 FN-AI-02\f0\fs22\cf1 : The system shall track human subjects (COCO Class 0) and maintain persistent tracking identities.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - \f1\fs19\cf2 FN-AI-03\f0\fs22\cf1 : The system shall classify bladed weapons and firearms using specialized threat weights.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - \f1\fs19\cf2 FN-AI-04\f0\fs22\cf1 : The system shall enforce multi-frame temporal confirmation across $N$ consecutive frames ($2 \\le N \\le 6$).\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - \f1\fs19\cf2 FN-AI-05\f0\fs22\cf1 : The system shall enforce an alert cooldown timer ($3\\text\{s\} \\le T_\{\\text\{cool\}\} \\le 60\\text\{s\}$) to eliminate duplicate alerts.\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 3.\b0  \b Forensic Evidence & Buffer Functions\b0 :\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - \f1\fs19\cf2 FN-EV-01\f0\fs22\cf1 : The system shall maintain a sliding circular memory buffer holding $3.0\\text\{ seconds\}$ of pre-roll frames.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - \f1\fs19\cf2 FN-EV-02\f0\fs22\cf1 : Upon verified threat detection, the system shall record $3.0\\text\{ seconds\}$ of post-roll frames.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - \f1\fs19\cf2 FN-EV-03\f0\fs22\cf1 : The system shall stitch and encode the $6.0\\text\{ second\}$ sequence into an MP4 clip on local disk.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - \f1\fs19\cf2 FN-EV-04\f0\fs22\cf1 : The system shall extract and store an annotated JPEG thumbnail showing the threat bounding box.\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 4.\b0  \b Incident Center & Triage Functions\b0 :\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - \f1\fs19\cf2 FN-INC-01\f0\fs22\cf1 : The system shall log all verified incidents into SQLite with timestamps and confidence scores.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - \f1\fs19\cf2 FN-INC-02\f0\fs22\cf1 : Authorized analysts shall update incident status: \f1\fs19\cf2 potential\f0\fs22\cf1 , \f1\fs19\cf2 acknowledged\f0\fs22\cf1 , \f1\fs19\cf2 reviewed\f0\fs22\cf1 , \f1\fs19\cf2 dismissed\f0\fs22\cf1 .\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - \f1\fs19\cf2 FN-INC-03\f0\fs22\cf1 : Authorized analysts shall attach and persist textual review notes to incident records.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - \f1\fs19\cf2 FN-INC-04\f0\fs22\cf1 : The system shall support multi-criteria incident filtering by category, status, and source camera.\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 5.\b0  \b Security Governance & Report Export Functions\b0 :\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - \f1\fs19\cf2 FN-REP-01\f0\fs22\cf1 : The system shall generate structured CSV exports of all incident records in the selected scope.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - \f1\fs19\cf2 FN-REP-02\f0\fs22\cf1 : The system shall generate structured JSON audit logs for external SIEM integration.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - \f1\fs19\cf2 FN-REP-03\f0\fs22\cf1 : The system shall enforce Role-Based Access Control clearance gates across all UI views and API endpoints.\par \pard\sa160\sb160\cf4 _____________________________________________________________________\cf1\par\sa200 \pard\sa200\sb140\f0\fs24\b\cf3 4.5 Non-Functional Requirements\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\li360\sa120\b 1.\b0  \b Performance & Latency Requirements\b0 :\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - The end-to-end inference latency for YOLOv8n CPU evaluation shall not exceed $35\\text\{ ms\}$ per frame on the recommended host CPU.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - The live MJPEG streaming broadcast latency to web clients shall not exceed $150\\text\{ ms\}$ on local LAN connections.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - Background batch video processing shall process footage at an effective throughput of at least $1.2\\times$ real-time speed.\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 2.\b0  \b Reliability & Availability Requirements\b0 :\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - The backend service shall maintain uninterrupted 24/7 operation with automatic recovery from transient camera socket errors.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - In the event of an unhandled exception in an individual camera worker thread, sibling camera worker threads must continue operating without interruption.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - SQLite WAL database writes must execute under ACID transaction guarantees, ensuring zero database corruption during unexpected system power loss.\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 3.\b0  \b Security & Privacy Requirements\b0 :\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - All video streams, evidence clips, and telemetry must remain strictly confined to the local host; no outbound external internet calls shall be initiated.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - Operator credentials and session tokens must be validated before granting access to privileged configuration actions.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - Passwords contained within RTSP URLs (e.g., \f1\fs19\cf2 rtsp://admin:pass@ip:port\f0\fs22\cf1 ) must be scrubbed and masked (e.g., \f1\fs19\cf2 rtsp://admin:***@ip:port\f0\fs22\cf1 ) before serialization in logs or frontend responses.\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 4.\b0  \b Usability & Human Factors Requirements\b0 :\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - The frontend Command Dashboard must adhere to high-contrast dark-mode styling (\f1\fs19\cf2 command-bg\f0\fs22\cf1  palette) optimized for security monitoring rooms.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - Live camera stream interruption must be clearly signaled via a visual reconnect overlay rather than presenting a static or blank screen.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - User action feedback (incident review, settings update, permission changes) must be confirmed via non-intrusive toast notifications within $200\\text\{ ms\}$.\par \pard\sa160\sb160\cf4 _____________________________________________________________________\cf1\par\sa200 \pard\sa240\sb180\f0\fs28\b\cf2 05 SYSTEM DESIGN\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa200\sb140\f0\fs24\b\cf3 5.1 Architectural Representation\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 The system is designed around a clean, multi-layered service-oriented architecture (SOA) emphasizing modularity, thread safety, and asynchronous concurrency:\par \pard\li360\ri360\f1\fs18\cf2\sa100 graph TD\par     subgraph Presentation Layer\par         UI["React 18 Single-Page Application (Vite + TypeScript)"]\par         DASH["Command Dashboard & Live Multi-View"]\par         INC["Incident Center & Triage Interface"]\par         SET["System Governance & RBAC Console"]\par     end\par \par     subgraph API Gateway Layer\par         FAST["FastAPI ASGI Server (Uvicorn @ Port 8000)"]\par         ROUT_CAM["/api/v1/cameras (Stream Management)"]\par         ROUT_VID["/api/v1/videos (Batch Analysis)"]\par         ROUT_INC["/api/v1/incidents (Incident Center & Evidence)"]\par         ROUT_SET["/api/v1/settings (Hyperparameters & RBAC)"]\par         ROUT_HLT["/api/v1/health (System Telemetry)"]\par     end\par \par     subgraph Core Processing & Computer Vision Layer\par         MGR["Camera Stream Manager (Multi-Threaded)"]\par         WORKER["Camera Worker Thread (Capture Loop)"]\par         DET["YOLOv8n Inference Engine (CPU PyTorch)"]\par         GATE["Temporal Multi-Frame Confirmation Gate"]\par         RING["Circular RAM Ring Buffer (3s Pre / 3s Post)"]\par         PROC["Batch Video Processor Thread"]\par     end\par \par     subgraph Data & Storage Layer\par         SQL[(SQLite 3 Database - WAL Mode)]\par         EVID["Evidence Vault (./storage/incidents/*.mp4)"]\par         THUMB["Thumbnail Vault (./storage/incidents/*.jpg)"]\par         MODELS["Model Weights Vault (./storage/models/*.pt)"]\par     end\par \par     UI --> FAST\par     DASH --> FAST\par     INC --> FAST\par     SET --> FAST\par \par     FAST --> ROUT_CAM\par     FAST --> ROUT_VID\par     FAST --> ROUT_INC\par     FAST --> ROUT_SET\par     FAST --> ROUT_HLT\par \par     ROUT_CAM <--> MGR\par     MGR --> WORKER\par     WORKER --> DET\par     WORKER --> GATE\par     WORKER --> RING\par     ROUT_VID --> PROC\par     PROC --> DET\par \par     ROUT_INC <--> SQL\par     ROUT_CAM <--> SQL\par     RING --> EVID\par     RING --> THUMB\par     DET --> MODELS\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa160\sb160\cf4 _____________________________________________________________________\cf1\par\sa200 \pard\sa200\sb140\f0\fs24\b\cf3 5.2 Architectural Description\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa160\sb100\f0\fs22\b\cf4 Layer 1: Ingestion and Preprocessing Pipeline\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 The ingestion layer is implemented within [\f1\fs19\cf2 backend/app/services/cameras/manager.py\f0\fs22\cf1 ](file:///c:/Users/sadik/Documents/antigravity/adventurous-raman/backend/app/services/cameras/manager.py). For each configured camera endpoint, the \f1\fs19\cf2 CameraStreamManager\f0\fs22\cf1  spawns a dedicated, daemonized background thread executing \f1\fs19\cf2 _capture_loop()\f0\fs22\cf1 . This thread interfaces directly with OpenCV's \f1\fs19\cf2 cv2.VideoCapture\f0\fs22\cf1  engine. When connecting to network RTSP streams, specific transport options are passed to enforce TCP transport (\f1\fs19\cf2 rtsp_transport=tcp\f0\fs22\cf1 ), eliminating packet drop and UDP frame tearing.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Frames are ingested at the camera's native frame rate (typically 25\u8211?30 FPS). The ingestion thread continuously updates \f1\fs19\cf2 self.latest_frame\f0\fs22\cf1  and appends a tuple of \f1\fs19\cf2 (timestamp, frame)\f0\fs22\cf1  to the circular \f1\fs19\cf2 self.rolling_buffer\f0\fs22\cf1 . To prevent CPU saturation, an adaptive sampling interval is enforced:\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 $$\\Delta t_\{\\text\{sample\}\} = \\frac\{1\}\{\\max(1, \\text\{SAMPLING\\_RATE\\_FPS\})\}$$\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Only when $(t_\{\\text\{current\}\} - t_\{\\text\{last\\_ai\}\}) \\ge \\Delta t_\{\\text\{sample\}\}$ does the frame pass to the AI inference pipeline, ensuring that preview rendering remains fluid while neural compute is throttled to 5\u8211?10 FPS.\par \pard\sa160\sb100\f0\fs22\b\cf4 Layer 2: Dual-Model Computer Vision Engine\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Within [\f1\fs19\cf2 backend/app/services/detection/detector.py\f0\fs22\cf1 ](file:///c:/Users/sadik/Documents/antigravity/adventurous-raman/backend/app/services/detection/detector.py), the \f1\fs19\cf2 ObjectDetector\f0\fs22\cf1  class encapsulates the Ultralytics YOLOv8n network. Upon initialization, the model weights (\f1\fs19\cf2 yolov8n.pt\f0\fs22\cf1  and \f1\fs19\cf2 yolov8n_weapon.pt\f0\fs22\cf1 ) are loaded from \f1\fs19\cf2 storage/models/\f0\fs22\cf1  into memory and pinned to CPU execution mode (\f1\fs19\cf2 device="cpu"\f0\fs22\cf1 ).\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 When a frame is dispatched for detection:\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 1.\b0  The image is converted from OpenCV BGR to RGB format.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\b 2.\b0  The image is resized to standard dimensions ($640 \\times 480$) using bilinear interpolation.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\b 3.\b0  The tensor is normalized to the floating-point range $[0.0, 1.0]$.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\b 4.\b0  The forward pass computes spatial feature pyramids through the C2f backbone and generates candidate bounding boxes with class probability distributions.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\b 5.\b0  Non-Maximum Suppression (NMS) is applied with an IoU threshold of $0.45$ to suppress redundant overlapping candidate boxes.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa160\sb100\f0\fs22\b\cf4 Layer 3: Temporal Confirmation & Ring Buffer Forensics\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 The temporal tracking subsystem ([\f1\fs19\cf2 backend/app/services/detection/weapon_tracker.py\f0\fs22\cf1 ](file:///c:/Users/sadik/Documents/antigravity/adventurous-raman/backend/app/services/detection/weapon_tracker.py)) monitors candidate detections across time. For each detected threat object, the system calculates centroid distance and spatial overlap against candidates in the preceding frame. If candidate detections maintain confidence $c \\ge \\tau_\{\\text\{conf\}\}$ across $K \\ge \\text\{WEAPON\\_CONSECUTIVE\\_FRAMES\}$ consecutive frames, an incident trigger is asserted.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Upon trigger assertion, the \f1\fs19\cf2 CameraStreamManager\f0\fs22\cf1  inspects its \f1\fs19\cf2 rolling_buffer\f0\fs22\cf1 . It extracts all buffered frames spanning $t_\{\\text\{trigger\}\} - 3.0\\text\{s\}$ to $t_\{\\text\{trigger\}\}$ (pre-roll) and initiates a post-roll recording thread that captures frames until $t_\{\\text\{trigger\}\} + 3.0\\text\{s\}$. The combined 6.0-second frame sequence is encoded into an MP4 container using OpenCV's \f1\fs19\cf2 VideoWriter\f0\fs22\cf1  with the \f1\fs19\cf2 avc1\f0\fs22\cf1  or \f1\fs19\cf2 mp4v\f0\fs22\cf1  codec. A representative frame at the peak confidence instant is saved as a JPEG thumbnail.\par \pard\sa160\sb100\f0\fs22\b\cf4 Layer 4: Relational Database Persistence (WAL Mode)\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 All persistent application state is managed by SQLAlchemy models mapped to SQLite 3 ([\f1\fs19\cf2 backend/app/models/database.py\f0\fs22\cf1 ](file:///c:/Users/sadik/Documents/antigravity/adventurous-raman/backend/app/models/database.py)). The database connection string initializes SQLite with asynchronous WAL mode:\par \pard\li360\ri360\f1\fs18\cf2\sa100 DATABASE_URL = "sqlite+aiosqlite:///./cctv_surveillance.db"\par PRAGMA journal_mode = WAL;\par PRAGMA synchronous = NORMAL;\par PRAGMA busy_timeout = 5000;\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Write-Ahead Logging writes transactional modifications to a separate \f1\fs19\cf2 cctv_surveillance.db-wal\f0\fs22\cf1  journal file, allowing concurrent reader processes to read uncommitted snapshots without encountering locks, guaranteeing sub-millisecond query response times for the frontend dashboard.\par \pard\sa160\sb100\f0\fs22\b\cf4 Layer 5: Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) Gateway\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Access governance is enforced across the application via [\f1\fs19\cf2 frontend/src/services/permissions.ts\f0\fs22\cf1 ](file:///c:/Users/sadik/Documents/antigravity/adventurous-raman/frontend/src/services/permissions.ts). Clearance checks are verified before rendering UI components and executing API mutations. The permissions matrix is dynamically managed by the Lead Commander through [\f1\fs19\cf2 Settings.tsx\f0\fs22\cf1 ](file:///c:/Users/sadik/Documents/antigravity/adventurous-raman/frontend/src/pages/Settings.tsx) and synchronized via local storage, enforcing strict separation of duties between supervisory commanders, investigative analysts, and field operators.\par \pard\sa160\sb160\cf4 _____________________________________________________________________\cf1\par\sa200 \pard\sa240\sb180\f0\fs28\b\cf2 06 METHODOLOGY\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa200\sb140\f0\fs24\b\cf3 6.1 Modules\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 The operational methodology is structured across four continuous, interacting phases:\par \pard\li360\ri360\f1\fs18\cf2\sa100 sequenceDiagram\par     participant Cam as CCTV / Camera Stream\par     participant Worker as Capture Loop (Manager)\par     participant AI as YOLOv8n CPU Engine\par     participant Gate as Temporal Gate & Ring Buffer\par     participant DB as SQLite WAL Database\par     participant UI as React Command Center\par \par     Cam->>Worker: Ingest Video Frame (25 FPS)\par     Worker->>Worker: Update Latest Frame & Append to 3s RAM Buffer\par     Worker->>AI: Dispatch Subsampled Frame (~5 FPS)\par     AI-->>Worker: Return Bounding Boxes & Confidence Scores\par     Worker->>Gate: Evaluate Candidate Weapons & People\par     alt Weapon Confirmed Across K Consecutive Frames\par         Gate->>Gate: Extract 3s Pre-Roll + 3s Post-Roll Frames\par         Gate->>Worker: Encode 6s MP4 Evidence & Thumbnail\par         Gate->>DB: Insert Incident & Evidence Records\par         Gate-->>UI: Real-Time Audio-Visual Threat Alert\par     end\par     Worker-->>UI: Broadcast Annotated MJPEG Stream\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\cf1 \pard\li360\sa120\b 1.\b0  \b Stream Ingestion & Worker Management Phase\b0 :\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - Camera configurations are queried from SQLite at application startup.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - For each camera, an ingestion worker thread is spawned.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - Non-blocking socket loops read raw frames, calculate actual incoming FPS, and detect connection loss.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - Frames are mirrored into an in-memory ring buffer holding 3 seconds of temporal history.\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 2.\b0  \b Perception & Threat Localization Phase\b0 :\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - Incoming frames are subsampled to the target AI processing rate (e.g., 5 FPS).\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - Frames are normalized and submitted to the YOLOv8n detector.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - The primary detector localizes human bodies, tracks spatial centroids, and updates current and unique occupancy counters.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - The secondary detector scans for bladed weapons and firearms within the scene.\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 3.\b0  \b Temporal Verification & Forensic Extraction Phase\b0 :\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - Detections are evaluated against temporal persistence criteria.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - If candidate weapons persist across consecutive frames, a verified security incident is declared.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - The system triggers pre-roll and post-roll video extraction from the circular buffer.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - An MP4 evidence clip and JPEG thumbnail are written to \f1\fs19\cf2 ./storage/incidents/\f0\fs22\cf1 .\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - An incident record is written to SQLite with review status set to \f1\fs19\cf2 potential\f0\fs22\cf1 .\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 4.\b0  \b Human-in-the-Loop Triage & Governance Phase\b0 :\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - Security operators receive real-time alert banners on the Command Dashboard.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - Authorized analysts open the Incident Center, inspect the synchronized 6-second evidence clip, and examine confidence scores.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - The analyst updates the incident status (\f1\fs19\cf2 acknowledged\f0\fs22\cf1 , \f1\fs19\cf2 reviewed\f0\fs22\cf1 , or \f1\fs19\cf2 dismissed\f0\fs22\cf1  as false positive) and records investigative notes.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 - Summary telemetry and auditable security reports are generated on demand.\par \pard\sa160\sb160\cf4 _____________________________________________________________________\cf1\par\sa200 \pard\sa200\sb140\f0\fs24\b\cf3 6.2 Algorithms Used and Model Selection\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa160\sb100\f0\fs22\b\cf4 YOLOv8n Model Architecture & Selection Rationale\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 YOLOv8n was selected as the perception backbone after benchmarking against alternative architectures (YOLOv5n, YOLOv7-tiny, MobileNetV3-SSD, and Faster R-CNN):\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 $$\\text\{mAP\}_\{50-95\} = \\int_\{0\}^\{1\} p(r) \\, dr$$\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 | Model Architecture | Parameter Count | GFLOPs ($640 \\times 640$) | CPU Latency (Intel i7-12700) | mAP ($0.50:0.95$) | Memory Footprint |\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 | \b Faster R-CNN (ResNet-50)\b0  | 41.5M | 180.0 | 280 ms (3.5 FPS) | 38.4% | ~850 MB |\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 | \b SSD-MobileNetV3\b0  | 4.8M | 12.5 | 45 ms (22 FPS) | 22.8% | ~120 MB |\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 | \b YOLOv5n\b0  | 1.9M | 4.5 | 22 ms (45 FPS) | 28.0% | ~80 MB |\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 | \b YOLOv8n (Selected)\b0  | \b 3.2M\b0  | \b 8.7\b0  | \b 18 ms (55 FPS single-thread)\b0  | \b 37.3%\b0  | \b ~95 MB\b0  |\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 YOLOv8n achieves a superior Pareto-optimal balance between computational efficiency and detection accuracy. Its anchor-free decoupled head eliminates hand-crafted anchor tuning, significantly enhancing recall for thin, elongated objects such as knives and small firearms held at diverse orientations.\par \pard\sa160\sb100\f0\fs22\b\cf4 Multi-Frame Confirmation & Temporal Gate Algorithm\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 The temporal confirmation gate operates as a discrete finite state machine:\par \pard\li360\ri360\f1\fs18\cf2\sa100 [Candidate Detection in Frame t]\par            \u9474?\par            \u9660?\par     Confidence >= Threshold ?\par       \u9500?\u9472?\u9472? No  \u9472?\u9472?\u9658? Reset Counter (k = 0)\par       \u9492?\u9472?\u9472? Yes \u9472?\u9472?\u9658? Overlap IoU >= 0.45 with Frame t-1 ?\par                     \u9500?\u9472?\u9472? No  \u9472?\u9472?\u9658? Reset Counter (k = 1)\par                     \u9492?\u9472?\u9472? Yes \u9472?\u9472?\u9658? Increment Counter (k = k + 1)\par                                   \u9474?\par                                   \u9660?\par                             k >= Consecutive Frames Required ?\par                               \u9500?\u9472?\u9472? No  \u9472?\u9472?\u9658? Maintain Candidate State\par                               \u9492?\u9472?\u9472? Yes \u9472?\u9472?\u9658? Assert Confirmed Threat Incident\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Let $D_t = \\\{d_1, d_2, \\dots, d_m\\\}$ denote the set of weapon detections at frame $t$, where each detection $d_i = (B_i, c_i, \\ell_i)$ comprises a bounding box $B_i = (x_1, y_1, x_2, y_2)$, a confidence score $c_i \\in [0, 1]$, and a class label $\\ell_i \\in \\\{\\text\{knife\}, \\text\{handgun\}, \\text\{rifle\}\\\}$.\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 A candidate track $T$ is updated by computing the spatial Intersection over Union (IoU) with detections in the subsequent frame $t+1$:\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 $$\\text\{IoU\}(B_a, B_b) = \\frac\{\\text\{Area\}(B_a \\cap B_b)\}\{\\text\{Area\}(B_a \\cup B_b)\}$$\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 A candidate detection is elevated to a confirmed security incident if and only if:\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 $$\\forall j \\in \\\{0, 1, \\dots, K-1\\\}, \\quad c_\{t-j\} \\ge \\tau_\{\\text\{conf\}\} \\quad \\land \\quad \\text\{IoU\}(B_\{t-j\}, B_\{t-j-1\}) \\ge \\tau_\{\\text\{IoU\}\}$$\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 $$\\text\{and\} \\quad (t_\{\\text\{current\}\} - t_\{\\text\{last\\_incident\}\}) \\ge T_\{\\text\{cooldown\}\}$$\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Where:\par \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  $\\tau_\{\\text\{conf\}\} = \\text\{WEAPON\\_CONFIDENCE\\_THRESHOLD\}$ (default: $0.35$).\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  $\\tau_\{\\text\{IoU\}\} = 0.45$.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  $K = \\text\{WEAPON\\_CONSECUTIVE\\_FRAMES\}$ (default: $2$).\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\bullet  $T_\{\\text\{cooldown\}\} = \\text\{EVENT\\_COOLDOWN\\_SECONDS\}$ (default: $10\\text\{s\}$).\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa160\sb100\f0\fs22\b\cf4 Automated Evidence Stitching Algorithm\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 The rolling buffer is implemented as a synchronized double-ended queue (deque) with a maximum capacity:\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 $$N_\{\\text\{max\}\} = \\lceil 3.0 \\times \\text\{FPS\}_\{\\text\{ingest\}\} \\rceil$$\par \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 When an incident triggers at time $t^*$:\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 1.\b0  The pre-roll sequence $S_\{\\text\{pre\}\} = \\\{F_i \\mid t^\i  - 3.0 \\le t(F_i) \\le t^\i0 \\\}$ is copied from the deque under mutex lock.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\b 2.\b0  The ingestion loop transitions to active capture mode, accumulating post-roll frames $S_\{\\text\{post\}\} = \\\{F_j \\mid t^\i  < t(F_j) \\le t^\i0  + 3.0\\\}$.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\li360\sa120\b 3.\b0  The concatenated sequence $S = S_\{\\text\{pre\}\} \\cup S_\{\\text\{post\}\}$ is serialized to disk via \f1\fs19\cf2 cv2.VideoWriter\f0\fs22\cf1 :\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 $$\\text\{VideoWriter\}(\\text\{filename\}, \\text\{fourcc\}('a','v','c','1'), \\text\{FPS\}_\{\\text\{target\}\}, (\\text\{width\}, \\text\{height\}))$$\par \pard\li360\sa120\b 4.\b0  An indexed JPEG thumbnail $T_\{\\text\{jpg\}\}$ is written using \f1\fs19\cf2 cv2.imwrite()\f0\fs22\cf1  at the frame corresponding to $\\max_\{F_i\}(c_i)$.\par\pard\sa200 \pard\sa160\sb160\cf4 _____________________________________________________________________\cf1\par\sa200 \pard\sa240\sb180\f0\fs28\b\cf2 07 IMPLEMENTATION\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa200\sb140\f0\fs24\b\cf3 7.1 Screen Shots & System Walkthrough\b0\par\sa200\fs22\cf1 \pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 The platform's user interface is structured across specialized operational modules accessible via the primary navigation sidebar:\par \pard\li360\ri360\f1\fs18\cf2\sa100